In C++, declaring a function as static means that the function belongs to the class rather than an instance of the class. This means that the function can be called without creating an object of the class. The static keyword affects the behavior of the function by restricting its access to only static data members and other static functions within the class.

Here is an example of erroneous code that involves using a static function in C++:

```cpp
