Here is an implementation of the stack with the additional requirements:

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class Stack {
    private int[] stack;
    private int top;
    private int min;
    private double median;

    public Stack() {
        stack = new int[1000];
        top = -1;
        min = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        median = 0.0;
    }

    public void push(int element) {
        if (top == stack.length - 1) {
            throw new StackOverflowException("Stack is full");
        }

        if (element < min) {
            stack[++top] = 2 * element - min;
            min = element;
        } else {
            stack[++top] = element;
        }

        updateMedian();
    }

    public int pop() {
        if (top == -1) {
            throw new StackUnderflowException("Stack is empty");
        }

        int element = stack[top--];

        if (element < min) {
            min = 2 * min - element;
        }

        updateMedian();

        return element;
    }

    public int getMinimum() {
        if (top == -1) {
            throw new StackUnderflowException("Stack is empty");
        }

        return min;
    }

    public double getMedian() {
        if (top == -1) {
            throw new StackUnderflowException("Stack is empty");
        }

        return median;
    }

    public void reverse() {
        int i = 0;
        int j = top;

        while (i < j) {
            int temp = stack[i];
            stack[i] = stack[j];
            stack[j] = temp;

            i++;
            j--;
        }
    }

    private void updateMedian() {
        if (top % 2 == 0) {
            median = stack[top / 2];
        } else {
            median = (stack[top / 2] + stack[top / 2 + 1]) / 2.0;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Stack stack = new Stack();
        stack.push(5);
        stack.push(10);
        stack.push(3);

        System.out.println("Minimum: " + stack.getMinimum());
        System.out.println("Median: " + stack.getMedian());

        stack.reverse();

        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(stack.stack));
    }
}

class StackOverflowException extends RuntimeException {
    public StackOverflowException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }
}

class StackUnderflowException extends RuntimeException {
    public StackUnderflowException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }
}
```

In this implementation, we use an array `stack` to store the elements, and the variable `top` keeps track of the index of the top element. We also have variables `min` and `median` to store the minimum and median values.

The `push` method adds an element to the stack. If the element is smaller than the current minimum, we store a modified version of the element in the array and update the minimum value. This modification allows us to retrieve the minimum value in O(1) time.

The `pop` method removes the top element from the stack and updates the minimum value if necessary.

The `getMinimum` and `getMedian` methods return the minimum and median values, respectively. The `reverse` method reverses the order of elements in the stack.

The `StackOverflowException` and `StackUnderflowException` are custom exceptions that are thrown when the stack is full or empty, respectively.

