In the C programming language, the "break" keyword is used to exit the innermost loop or switch statement. It allows you to prematurely terminate the loop or switch and move the control flow to the next statement after the loop or switch.

On the other hand, the "continue" keyword is used to skip the current iteration of a loop and move on to the next iteration. It allows you to bypass the remaining code within the loop body for the current iteration and move to the next iteration.

Let's consider an example where both "break" and "continue" are used within a loop to manipulate an array. Suppose we have an array of positive integers called "arr" and we want to find the first occurrence of a number divisible by 5 and print it. If we encounter a negative number, we should skip that iteration and move to the next one.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int arr[] = {10, 2, 8, -3, 15, 20};
    int size = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);

    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        if (arr[i] < 0) {
            continue;  // Skip negative numbers
        }

        if (arr[i] % 5 == 0) {
            printf("First number divisible by 5: %d\n", arr[i]);
            break;  // Exit the loop after finding the first number divisible by 5
        }
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the loop iterates through the array elements. If an element is negative, the "continue" statement skips that iteration and moves to the next element. If an element is divisible by 5, the "break" statement terminates the loop and the program prints the message indicating the first occurrence of a number divisible by 5. In this case, the output will be "First number divisible by 5: 10".

The time complexity of this algorithm is O(n), where n is the number of elements in the array. The loop iterates through all the elements in the worst case scenario.

The space complexity of this algorithm is O(1), as it only uses a fixed amount of memory to store the array and loop variables, regardless of the size of the input array.

